#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124458 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124262 :question/id #uuid "bf35c502-5a22-5953-9158-b2464d28c09f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3377" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd2b430e-fd5d-57ba-bc28-f7c113eda07a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954 :topic/math-academy-id 177 :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Components"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143 :topic/math-academy-id 1934 :topic/title "The Gradient Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988 :topic/math-academy-id 176 :topic/title "Calculating the Magnitude of Cartesian Vectors in 2D"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e3020e-b220-554e-a3f0-07e483342e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122373} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e6986e-ec79-5752-9e0d-ea5960beab16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122374} {:answer/id #uuid "a8340c19-e527-5c1b-953c-c260766408b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{21}}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122375} {:answer/id #uuid "db0760d0-b134-5ae3-af68-54c39c2f1781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122376} {:answer/id #uuid "0801896c-1c2b-510b-86df-c0542a7bf4e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14\\sqrt{29}}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122377}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfb4078-c321-5580-bb20-9356ac667745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122372}] :question/id #uuid "4ba373e3-c7d5-54ce-a51c-b2950c874b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-36273" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + y^{2}$ at the point $(0, 1)$ in the direction of the vector $v = 5i + 2j$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93fcdd41-5aba-54fd-9f19-d383cd0c8b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{78}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122775} {:answer/id #uuid "abdb47d5-6a5e-50f3-b8d6-ae7cb6744ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122776} {:answer/id #uuid "b32de488-c397-50e7-9e0d-a9d2fa222187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122777} {:answer/id #uuid "5be9dc56-00c0-5419-8e09-cdc3986dabdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122778} {:answer/id #uuid "6f19ba24-1488-5e98-a493-56f081245961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{78}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122779}] :answer-field/id #uuid "52446f3c-8c33-54d6-a7e9-42d22d6cabce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122774}] :question/id #uuid "f655da5c-6e4d-590f-afb2-3ac467aa95f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37077" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x, y) = (x + y^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-2)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2, 2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eaac021-36b6-54cc-b7ec-c212007fad51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eaac021-36b6-54cc-b7ec-c212007fad51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234738} :answer-field/id #uuid "61ede809-36cc-5d59-b1d8-f7a0b29d9d5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e02c41aa-7da1-51d0-a8bf-4d3360c07bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-168116" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\text{div}\\,V$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required directional derivative is ${f}_{u}^{′}(1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n∇\\,f(1,1)=∇(\\text{div}\\,V)\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=4\\,i+2\\,j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(4\\,i+2\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}(-3\\,i+4\\,j)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 4}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08151725-c767-5bfa-8cc0-566dac73c7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263073} {:answer/id #uuid "6b75a71e-ff62-5063-9c04-4318296a44fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263928} {:answer/id #uuid "8f591e1b-9777-5d0f-b497-43f656adfa40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264230} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6ad8b4-a673-5008-ae07-fbef7600a1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264645} {:answer/id #uuid "bd888fe4-7f5e-51b1-a53d-363189aedc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08151725-c767-5bfa-8cc0-566dac73c7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263073} :answer-field/id #uuid "93ddfe34-8e2a-5c0d-ad1d-5d50a7d04569" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e9cc9e7-1d62-51d1-8fa2-dd51fa7daa28" :question/math-academy-id "q-130257" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,1) & =i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,1) & =∇\\,f(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da1714c-2291-5808-ac0e-0808ab462f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263272} {:answer/id #uuid "450b63f8-2ed8-5c35-8a78-b5c67477b5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263607} {:answer/id #uuid "72992976-c623-506b-9f6f-3a6145bc7b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263992} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc54a79-4160-5b82-ac56-cae08fe005df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264084} {:answer/id #uuid "b7690e09-485b-5e3c-b1cf-05000b42442a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72992976-c623-506b-9f6f-3a6145bc7b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263992} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8b53a4-366c-5e74-a168-e6f6544ed5ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265461}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35885778-6551-5cbf-9f14-c659423ae334" :question/math-academy-id "q-130260" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ at the point $(0,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3\\,i+4\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,-1) & =∇\\,f(0,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086fecd5-a126-5f32-9308-1bc895c050b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263080} {:answer/id #uuid "8d43de9a-94e9-5878-939e-2733944f61c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264208} {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} {:answer/id #uuid "b92952ce-92d6-5fe1-84dc-4e5ec973c535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264605} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1aafca-14b7-5d95-9816-988f20570192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b78ab4c-27f6-5a87-8918-b6215e38243b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264329} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1d9c83-156f-56e6-8ce6-3d01bf086064" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae068a0a-3a23-5516-a4b7-13c70903e5db" :question/math-academy-id "q-130263" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=i-j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy)j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)-1)\\,i+(2(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,f(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i-j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(1)+1(-1)}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00835dd1-8061-59e4-8040-48a957847175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321154} {:answer/id #uuid "2b84de9a-1d96-5d76-b304-fcc568ae08c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321481} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8d9dd6-ab7c-531c-92df-429bb88b6255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322286} {:answer/id #uuid "93873ce7-adfb-5826-85f5-744afae73ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322319} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e6066c-22d2-5127-a385-fb4a7da7ae82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4e6066c-22d2-5127-a385-fb4a7da7ae82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323067} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ab9660-72ee-5cec-b767-3be5f30759da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323617}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cd131d3-dbf5-532a-a1c1-f69d113636a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36290" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y$ at the point $A(1,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,1⟩-⟨1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,0) & =⟨{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(1,0) & =∇f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1304514c-b435-522f-ad3a-591f2559a22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351730} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4fba7a-fe7c-5a9c-9fbe-87782d23df70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\sqrt{43}}{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352676} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} {:answer/id #uuid "c94460ac-494e-513e-82ed-c599cb03d029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{43}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353320} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e9e528-e824-57bb-899a-9e75210a8db6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8a1b502-7fbf-5960-aaec-5f60565ea22e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353314} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f8833b-3f19-5c49-a196-1b1b54521800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8f45df0-069c-5e4a-a4d7-bdd2e15f5467" :question/math-academy-id "q-86429" :question/problem "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}+2y$ at the point $(0,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3i+5j.$" :question/worked-solution "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}(3i+5j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =12x\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(0,1) & =(12\\cdot 0)\\,i+2\\,j \\\\  & =2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇\\,f(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =2j\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}i+\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{34}}j) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Directional Derivative"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124459 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :question/id #uuid "dc1e4548-ce72-5618-86f4-b68b46b50550" :question/math-academy-id "e-3381" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff351d3f-449b-5c41-8f72-d1917b2b7536" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8f5286b-99bb-5a1a-927f-88c70551e130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin (1)i + 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122379} {:answer/id #uuid "da96497d-aef7-5188-aa0a-fd775e4f774d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2i - j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122380} {:answer/id #uuid "e735c0a2-12f8-53a9-8832-06b822e65e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 - \\sin (1))i + j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122381} {:answer/id #uuid "fb69c42b-63e6-5bbc-ad18-f9fbc6438e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sin (1) - 2)i - j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122382} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2b4fe4-fb54-51f9-b481-aa6353b4c03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i + j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122383}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0e3d8181-5a0a-57e3-b64b-0cfbd5cc5890" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122378}] :question/id #uuid "e75dd59c-9b9f-50d9-a9a4-c1850c66ee84" :question/math-academy-id "q-36293" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x, y) = \\cos x + x^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1, 1)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ff85bb-8ce2-5025-a0a1-83fc98417ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2\\pi ,-2,-\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321217} {:answer/id #uuid "636513d9-9ad2-5bbe-b8b3-cd67a4bf8d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321920} {:answer/id #uuid "9f98fa5a-9408-52f3-8b7e-8f25e3ab2ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi ,-1,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322407} {:answer/id #uuid "c672059e-d68f-556c-9a70-117d11270e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322686} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb1fca-19f0-5446-8266-9a4f0c5b2778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2\\pi ,2,\\pi ⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322752} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3b7500-df79-56f9-843a-ede4ff70a4be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a412c3a-6b92-5f8b-8cf9-ec76f9ca97fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-36305" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z),\\,2y,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x+\\pi z)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(1,1,2) & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2),\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi \\cdot 2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ),\\,2,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2\\pi ,\\,2,\\,\\pi ⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371188b1-b7ec-57b7-b295-164f4505e38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263494} {:answer/id #uuid "468d5107-6a48-54fc-a274-623f490c7ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263617} {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9a260e-6d34-52c4-a50b-774f0edf8d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263781} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f338bb-07ad-5dd5-a554-45630ad32b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5224940d-41f5-5791-9620-631ff60b6036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,i+10\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263707} :answer-field/id #uuid "82b1bccb-f245-5952-bbe0-74fd47a6963c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265475}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "234c72df-7b34-5698-b613-f1acebf8fd8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130276" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xy+2{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+3y)\\,i+(3x+4y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(2(2)+3(1))\\,i+(3(2)+4(1))\\,j \\\\  & =7\\,i+10\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $7\\,i+10\\,j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "255172e6-7a9f-58d5-a331-2f9b1c75fdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,-\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321437} {:answer/id #uuid "ad71c1c5-8c51-5b0e-a1c2-00e6be326690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,\\pi ,\\,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322506} {:answer/id #uuid "b2583193-7c57-5a7b-a812-d2606cdf10e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,\\,0,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322542} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d64c08-23f9-5296-9b84-8bb3edc8f8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322698} {:answer/id #uuid "dd715aea-f494-54b7-886a-982c585d2245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7d64c08-23f9-5296-9b84-8bb3edc8f8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322698} :answer-field/id #uuid "511dda4a-e46b-5ff9-8563-b0b60e9db9a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323325}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e292b5a-7460-5589-a8da-d2424c37287d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37080" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x$, in what direction does the function increase most rapidly at the point $(1,0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2}x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y)+3{z}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x+y),\\,6xz⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0)+3\\cdot {1}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1+0),\\,6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨-\\pi +3,\\,-1,\\,6⟩.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2d1818-23b6-5bc6-9705-1aa1f8fcde6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{-1}-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349013} {:answer/id #uuid "68eb9247-b932-5869-944e-6c106bd7cb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\text{ }i+ej" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349413} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd17f28-20e2-5ce1-8906-d302e5e5f909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}+1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349472} {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349749} {:answer/id #uuid "df06fa9d-9784-5210-b854-98e07a5502ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e+1)\\text{ }i+(e-1)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91bd11c8-e407-5439-bdc4-1470bfd9ed1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349749} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ce0ac9-9872-520a-b868-69cd398be0c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350832}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e724341-d4b7-5d33-9112-e9d06eaccd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-83554" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy$ increase most rapidly at the point $(-1,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y{e}^{x}-y)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{x}{y}+{e}^{x}-x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1\\cdot {e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{-1}{1}+{e}^{-1}-(-1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $({e}^{-1}-1)\\text{ }i+{e}^{-1}j.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351772} {:answer/id #uuid "215ecffb-b146-5627-91b0-550f671dd2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351867} {:answer/id #uuid "722664ed-33e9-5cc1-b19b-4351c6efb19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352566} {:answer/id #uuid "81a3d5cd-c8fc-5c45-bcca-96eb05f8c947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352695} {:answer/id #uuid "df442e6f-48af-5860-ac3c-76c823ada575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1721b07e-259c-5a3f-a8e9-4f2d59c63517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351772} :answer-field/id #uuid "40e73d7d-cb49-50d9-9ae1-9e40c00f9a4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353927}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1b68e57-d9ea-56e4-b3b2-4bb6dcbf9139" :question/math-academy-id "q-86537" :question/problem "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $-j.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction of Maximum Increase or Maximum Decrease of a Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124460 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :question/id #uuid "1eea4610-508a-5437-802e-9d009b777e82" :question/math-academy-id "e-3382" :question/problem "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c330648f-69ad-5778-98cb-bd48a0b160dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8377f097-45c2-5136-bb1a-ec94780f46ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122385} {:answer/id #uuid "85e45877-a3bb-5df2-b95b-1b22f8f282b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122386} {:answer/id #uuid "c53a2aeb-cf40-56b1-837e-e4a412f6db98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122387} {:answer/id #uuid "42f3f813-ee54-55f9-9e98-8cd208a09b33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122388} {:answer/id #uuid "4a7e03b0-95c8-5010-8940-20600f4e7159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122389}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ff58ae13-957c-59b0-9fda-ff457d0f11e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122384}] :question/id #uuid "a985e88b-265b-5691-956a-877ec947cdff" :question/math-academy-id "q-36297" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x, y) = \\frac{x^{2}}{y}$. What is the rate of change of $g(x, y)$ at the point $(2, 1)$ in the direction of the vector $v = ⟨ - 1, 3⟩$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4fb74f0-7cbb-50ca-b586-f737d6776ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{π + e}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122769} {:answer/id #uuid "dc78535a-8c2f-56df-9b87-429982d864c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{π + e}{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122770} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fd833e-3cf7-55af-bc07-55bc949b0cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{π^{2} + e^{2}}}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122771} {:answer/id #uuid "39f8b7b1-abe0-5cdc-8e14-a6a2092e6f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{π^{4} + e^{2}}}{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122772} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9b409c-8615-5646-8312-02eb29a5d380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{π^{2} + e^{2}}}{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122773}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4aca3088-e8f0-5dbc-95a5-523c9e4320d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122768}] :question/id #uuid "8ea3ebc5-2c81-5696-aab4-931973a19233" :question/math-academy-id "q-37074" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x, y) = \\sin (y\\ln x)$. What is the rate of change of $g(x, y)$ at the point $(e, π)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5cc75b-1aa1-5b30-9362-5f8752961da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263250} {:answer/id #uuid "1d98f894-779c-5822-84b3-85b9a53ad6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263271} {:answer/id #uuid "4efb98a8-8a82-5879-9aee-9a09d6afe439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263687} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb30fc7-6c64-5f56-9344-f70ad5eab684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264636} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a55fec-f5cc-59e3-8bae-75fcaf8ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a55fec-f5cc-59e3-8bae-75fcaf8ea6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265001} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ef6581-d8af-5b79-a84d-4f8dc281dbef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265678}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "129e769a-9e81-5bb8-a357-71dd7d678811" :question/math-academy-id "q-130801" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(-1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{y}{x})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}},\\frac{1}{(-1)}-\\frac{(-1)}{{1}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,-\\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0(1)+0(-\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323791 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f931965-4230-53c9-9c4e-51f593f9d015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321267} {:answer/id #uuid "57f67efd-2b45-5e67-a5e4-4b0fd709ba1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321837} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1a8d46-fbc3-5f92-8b69-6709f7023c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322114} {:answer/id #uuid "cda4641b-3880-5a2f-a60a-2d4b84c1090a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322750} {:answer/id #uuid "d4216551-4828-50f1-870a-15d1ba740ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4216551-4828-50f1-870a-15d1ba740ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{82}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322802} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e6c5b32-a96f-564b-b174-aa4099e65f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323476}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a13cba05-5f4a-5d75-8f65-f72eb54e0d73" :question/math-academy-id "q-36300" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2xy+{e}^{3x-y}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(2y+3{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }i-(2x-{e}^{3x-y})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(1,3) & =-(2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }i-(2\\cdot 1-{e}^{3\\cdot 1-3})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,3)$ in the direction of $-∇f(1,3)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(1,3)‖ & =‖-9\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{82}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8837b541-d3d8-5ebe-8ea8-af61591cb6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322232} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f03f12-e1ac-5472-a11e-f1f87bcd9621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322572} {:answer/id #uuid "c1717e43-53c5-54f5-8c5f-68df4b9cfd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322650} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ef68e3-e12b-57d6-890b-83987a1922bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2a54f97-4039-5587-a94f-458a55a1776d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322792} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a40605a-36ce-555d-abab-e5a81e672aeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a0c033d-5079-51f1-a44e-c13c59694293" :question/math-academy-id "q-37083" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y).$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇\\,f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x+y)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇\\,f(2,-1) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+1)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(2,-1)$ in the direction of $-∇f(2,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇\\,f(2,-1)‖ & =‖-i-j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2209d039-cd49-5b32-a5a2-fccba4c4ad04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348739} {:answer/id #uuid "2f701612-723b-57b5-8841-6b161d28a285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348867} {:answer/id #uuid "aa56a022-8acc-58dd-9945-dec0f389f84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349960} {:answer/id #uuid "b2decfd4-db20-5142-a782-96a864cc1cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350030} {:answer/id #uuid "eedbad12-eb1d-52b0-b11b-38c97411879b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f701612-723b-57b5-8841-6b161d28a285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348867} :answer-field/id #uuid "b045770b-be7d-51e7-a88e-a4035b50e4fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e60c4cce-b8b4-5a37-8e5f-19c98e4b5e86" :question/math-academy-id "q-83608" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-2,2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1)$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{y}+\\pi y,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{{y}^{2}}+\\pi x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(1,1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{1}+\\pi \\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)}{{1}^{2}}+\\pi \\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi +\\pi ,0+\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =∇\\,g(1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨0,\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+\\pi \\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351521 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023898fb-a616-56d4-822f-050c9d7ed901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348471} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1d16bc-4349-5f72-b0c9-a69b046c4e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348560} {:answer/id #uuid "131969f5-9f12-5738-8e7a-92fb804ff6ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348611} {:answer/id #uuid "92759975-27e9-56dd-8a00-cf955f71c7f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349756} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e6ddfc-23e4-505d-a08b-5cfa9e448be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "023898fb-a616-56d4-822f-050c9d7ed901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348471} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4a8d4eb-f7c9-5c5f-88e7-3850845e3558" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351109}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54b4a6ea-a43d-5fa2-b223-b9c52e67cdd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83613" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ What is the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of the vector $v=⟨-1,1⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the direction of $v$ is equal to the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient $∇\\,g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡y-2y{sec}^{2}⁡x,\\,2x{sec}^{2}⁡y-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot 1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\,\\pi -2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the directional derivative ${g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =∇\\,g(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2-\\pi ,\\pi -2⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+(\\pi -2)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi -4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(\\pi -2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rate of Change of a Function in a Given Direction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124461 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050143 :topic/math-academy-id 1934 :topic/title "The Gradient Vector"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988 :topic/math-academy-id 176 :topic/title "Calculating the Magnitude of Cartesian Vectors in 2D"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93dcc0e-bacf-58c5-b6f4-47f43da168f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122415} {:answer/id #uuid "28b09048-1131-506e-a285-e59ac557f01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122416} {:answer/id #uuid "370200a2-bd3f-5447-b9b1-ea23d53237b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122417} {:answer/id #uuid "42dfcda5-2b9e-563b-b4e1-fb6d44bf17e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122418} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6f5f06-d20e-5856-b8b1-1ac758877073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i - j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122419}] :answer-field/id #uuid "37f65865-99f1-54aa-953e-7c631fab08cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122414}] :question/id #uuid "9e917aee-92ff-5d58-8526-d5d5bb6b5240" :question/math-academy-id "q-36311" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x, y) = 2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - y$ that passes through the point where $(x, y) = (0, 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f27d5774-1d7f-5ab4-89fe-a21d12c54d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7i - 12j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122469} {:answer/id #uuid "0c98f74f-ddb3-5180-b0de-0967ea8e4636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7i + 7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122470} {:answer/id #uuid "28fd0a19-0ae3-560f-bf90-dd64e91b6777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i + 2j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122471} {:answer/id #uuid "4079226d-5808-50e6-a05b-af629030f084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i - 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122472} {:answer/id #uuid "4165f948-3799-5c70-a272-874ae43fc465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2i + j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122473}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b33a5905-3216-54dd-ad86-072ac416bdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122468}] :question/id #uuid "5e2999c4-1892-5dd4-a533-5ed9b93e52c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-36378" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + 4y + 5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x, y) = (2, 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1441a2d6-6646-53cf-910b-640be6a3ce12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263189} {:answer/id #uuid "1d83c4d7-e29e-518f-8d81-fc678900ebe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263270} {:answer/id #uuid "3151187a-5967-53d5-ae4b-7ac589aab55a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263421} {:answer/id #uuid "5d332831-6792-53d3-9b0e-7703fcddae9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263808} {:answer/id #uuid "970e8c1f-d7a6-5f66-9d87-f248bc2ef3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1441a2d6-6646-53cf-910b-640be6a3ce12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263189} :answer-field/id #uuid "2376d46b-16ff-5b21-93b2-796a7646d0b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265308}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3aa96b9c-802f-5254-a50f-7fd477588b2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130271" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,4)}{‖∇f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "478e5238-b82e-52a7-b916-e3176ac5c7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321709} {:answer/id #uuid "64e34a4f-d3ec-50b3-936b-5c2fc27ddda9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321930} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c06b6f-03ce-5422-811e-bdf364365b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322577} {:answer/id #uuid "cd17be78-1f08-5dc6-b596-77159cdc94ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322743} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f19b61-a2d2-5713-a47b-f64a9abfebff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8f19b61-a2d2-5713-a47b-f64a9abfebff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322974} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d79ebc-11b5-5629-a6fc-dd3e1f57e714" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323599}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51e044b0-3c0e-5644-831b-0d5fd92a4af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-36346" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(1,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(1,1)}{‖∇g(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{i+j}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157bc3da-2d1c-5137-b516-d8ff7c42bad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336235} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfe7ebf-3262-597f-b2fd-edf534fd618c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336607} {:answer/id #uuid "4baa559e-cde4-5072-9145-09ac20cb641a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} {:answer/id #uuid "829583d7-8e78-579d-9221-64ce8b48dc24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337220} {:answer/id #uuid "e26af925-4997-53e5-8b3d-7425b92d8e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4baa559e-cde4-5072-9145-09ac20cb641a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336735} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48649d2-bf76-55fc-aef9-b0aff8e82c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338680}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22cfc584-e8e6-55e0-a1d3-35d71c5742a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71370" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }i+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049b7f23-d6a7-5be3-a6ca-ac19b8a84234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336070} {:answer/id #uuid "123c36c6-7310-581c-8de4-b675e090dc56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336203} {:answer/id #uuid "4b49d759-d015-5843-b6e9-3497dc09edfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336730} {:answer/id #uuid "6e16ffd6-406d-50b5-9b6a-0f4b899fc6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd3f738-ef19-5d09-afcb-04a5b6bdd7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(-2i+i)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e16ffd6-406d-50b5-9b6a-0f4b899fc6bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337009} :answer-field/id #uuid "60184a90-8959-560b-aa4a-378830d13dbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "436e814d-020d-5b28-b909-495bbb1c431a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71374" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x+3y-2xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{ }i+(3-2x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{ }i+(3-2(\\frac{3}{2}))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{‖∇f(\\frac{3}{2},1)‖} & =\\frac{-i}{1}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2372ab35-9f15-566a-9150-808733fd4bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336363} {:answer/id #uuid "2630b03f-d66e-5733-b918-78baaf9f7ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336392} {:answer/id #uuid "e77b993b-c3bb-5f5a-9474-9bd52741e5b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338055} {:answer/id #uuid "e9097253-bc27-5ce4-8fb8-daded67d09c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338066} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a289db-1c93-5c45-ab8f-caa51a0ee7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a289db-1c93-5c45-ab8f-caa51a0ee7e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338195} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d94b131-873d-56a9-92e8-a2ed25af773a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338331}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1092dcda-9470-51be-bb7f-4be55bcc6069" :question/math-academy-id "q-71381" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}+x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2i+2j}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i+2j}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1239f96f-2e4e-508d-a9f2-75796efa86a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}(4\\,i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383155} {:answer/id #uuid "45a77767-f45c-5c88-af82-5221a5fbd33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383156} {:answer/id #uuid "33650346-32bd-534a-a64c-45e97e73fd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,j-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383157} {:answer/id #uuid "64e6389b-f8ce-50bf-a097-05a8b301d96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\,i-\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383158} {:answer/id #uuid "d73cf648-7973-5ea5-aece-b31b3cb3d9d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}(2\\,i+4\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a77767-f45c-5c88-af82-5221a5fbd33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383156} :answer-field/id #uuid "876e06ec-0ab3-5cfb-b188-15f5d0a5be15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383154}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cee7213b-050a-5cea-8e38-6fcbc59025d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129892" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x+y}{x-y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x+y}{x-y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(-1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-2)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-2,-1)}{‖∇f(-2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2\\,i-4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce66abc2-1493-50b5-a986-b312bd69b0d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383162} {:answer/id #uuid "97a0676c-47bb-533c-80d9-c0d75ef46077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383163} {:answer/id #uuid "83aa1130-305c-5cb1-ac5b-814ca52426a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383164} {:answer/id #uuid "21c379b6-9115-5238-9915-dbace494a5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383165} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bd01d5-5f84-5141-bac1-7784075d4ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83aa1130-305c-5cb1-ac5b-814ca52426a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383164} :answer-field/id #uuid "20194f88-b71b-5ea3-a75b-4873d6a1dd62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383161}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9318eb5-4446-512c-876e-9868eec1bc8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130274" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}))j \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})}{1+{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{1+{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8}\\,i+\\frac{2}{8}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,-2)}{‖∇f(2,-2)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{4}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79312bdb-f048-5202-b77a-86cf88a3b986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i+4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385510} {:answer/id #uuid "8d705007-aa0c-5cde-a573-738e2aff9dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385511} {:answer/id #uuid "03fdb318-277a-5b7b-bf02-538ad5ca1565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385512} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c5a255-ae95-5c0d-ae54-45b0dbd4189d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385513} {:answer/id #uuid "ab101cb8-1cc9-52ea-95e4-ec2e563d3dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab101cb8-1cc9-52ea-95e4-ec2e563d3dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385514} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d844bc-8ba7-554c-ae01-efce8b8ec0bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385509}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "522c74bc-3c1b-5bd8-a1b2-9e3f5a7b89a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71383" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4)$, can be found by computing $∇f(5,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(5,4) & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(5,4)}{‖∇f(5,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve"}]}
